1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,448
Monday morning, in the mobile bank team.

2
00:00:02,498 --> 00:00:07,824
Jasur, an iOS developer, gets his task: connect the app to heeyID.

3
00:00:07,874 --> 00:00:12,840
He searches for the heeyID SDK, a package that he can add with one line.

4
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:14,712
There is none.

5
00:00:14,762 --> 00:00:20,624
So how does a team build against heeyID with no SDK?

6
00:00:20,674 --> 00:00:25,040
In Lesson 8, every key stayed in the Vault of the bank, and none reached a phone.

7
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:31,640
This lesson shows what reaches the apps instead: a contract, a guide, and a short list of things that an app may keep.

8
00:00:31,690 --> 00:00:34,304
First, why there is no SDK.

9
00:00:34,354 --> 00:00:37,160
The teams of the bank build the apps of the bank.

10
00:00:37,210 --> 00:00:44,168
An SDK from heeyID would add work before the first release, and a second release train for every app.

11
00:00:44,218 --> 00:00:52,112
I think no SDK is the honest choice for a first release: the contract is the product, and each team owns its own code.

12
00:00:52,162 --> 00:00:54,248
So heeyID gives three things.

13
00:00:54,298 --> 00:00:56,120
The REST API.

14
00:00:56,170 --> 00:00:59,120
The OpenAPI contract that describes it.

15
00:00:59,170 --> 00:01:02,120
And an integration guide with the rules for the apps.

16
00:01:02,170 --> 00:01:08,048
The contract describes every call: the path, the request, the answer and the errors.

17
00:01:08,098 --> 00:01:19,544
There is one call for each step: start a registration, verify the code, set the password, log in, approve a login, give consent, open a proofing session and renew a session.

18
00:01:19,594 --> 00:01:25,976
Each request carries a correlation identifier, and after registration also the device identifier.

19
00:01:26,026 --> 00:01:34,472
Each error has one shape: a code, a reason key, a message for the customer, and whether a retry is allowed, and after how long.

20
00:01:34,522 --> 00:01:38,024
The codes are the error outcomes of the requirements, one to one.

21
00:01:38,074 --> 00:01:42,416
So the app of Jasur never reads a sentence to find out what went wrong.

22
00:01:42,466 --> 00:01:45,176
It reads the code, and shows the message.

23
00:01:45,226 --> 00:01:47,936
Then come the SDKs that do exist.

24
00:01:47,986 --> 00:01:56,192
The bank adds the MyID SDK and the TAF SDK to the master app, and only to the master app.

25
00:01:56,242 --> 00:01:59,072
MyID runs as a native check inside the app.

26
00:01:59,122 --> 00:02:05,624
The backend opens the session, and the customer never leaves the app: no web view, and no web frame.

27
00:02:05,674 --> 00:02:08,672
The MyID credential never goes to the phone.

28
00:02:08,722 --> 00:02:13,376
Each app also gets the push SDK of the bank, to receive the wake-up signals.

29
00:02:13,426 --> 00:02:15,248
Now the guide.

30
00:02:15,298 --> 00:02:20,072
Its most important page is a table: what an app can keep, and where.

31
00:02:20,122 --> 00:02:24,752
The device identifier, in the storage that the Keychain or the Keystore protects.

32
00:02:24,802 --> 00:02:30,080
The refresh token, in the Keychain, or in storage that the Android Keystore protects.

33
00:02:30,130 --> 00:02:36,920
The access token and the identity summary, in memory only, until they expire or the app closes.

34
00:02:36,970 --> 00:02:42,272
An approval request, in memory and on the screen, until Aziz answers it.

35
00:02:42,322 --> 00:02:44,144
The push token.

36
00:02:44,194 --> 00:02:48,968
The app PIN, only as a slow, salted hash, on the device.

37
00:02:49,018 --> 00:02:50,840
And the language.

38
00:02:50,890 --> 00:02:53,744
Then the other column: what an app never keeps.

39
00:02:53,794 --> 00:02:55,616
The password.

40
00:02:55,666 --> 00:02:57,272
The MyID credential.

41
00:02:57,322 --> 00:03:00,032
The MyID result code, after its one upload.

42
00:03:00,082 --> 00:03:02,000
The personal identification number.

43
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:04,904
Document data, addresses and images.

44
00:03:04,954 --> 00:03:07,616
A one-time code, after Aziz types it.

45
00:03:07,666 --> 00:03:09,824
A limit that the gateway uses.

46
00:03:09,874 --> 00:03:11,696
And biometric data.

47
00:03:11,746 --> 00:03:13,568
The row about limits matters.

48
00:03:13,618 --> 00:03:15,992
An app may show a counter, for comfort.

49
00:03:16,042 --> 00:03:20,120
But the platform applies every limit itself, on the server.

50
00:03:20,170 --> 00:03:23,672
A limit in the app is only a convenience, never a control.

51
00:03:23,722 --> 00:03:26,024
Now be honest about the edge of this design.

52
00:03:26,074 --> 00:03:28,976
heeyID cannot enforce these rules inside an app.

53
00:03:29,026 --> 00:03:31,904
It cannot look into the Keychain on the phone of Aziz.

54
00:03:31,954 --> 00:03:36,176
So the guide gives the rules, and the API tests keep the apps correct.

55
00:03:36,226 --> 00:03:39,488
Each app is a client of the contract, and nothing more.

56
00:03:39,538 --> 00:03:41,360
Back to Jasur.

57
00:03:41,410 --> 00:03:49,904
By Wednesday, his app calls the contract, keeps the device identifier in the Keychain and the access token in memory, and shows the errors by code.

58
00:03:49,954 --> 00:03:55,928
He adds the MyID and TAF SDKs, and nothing from heeyID.

59
00:03:55,978 --> 00:04:01,424
When the contract changes, the OpenAPI file shows the change first.

60
00:04:01,474 --> 00:04:05,888
To recap: there is no heeyID SDK in this release.

61
00:04:05,938 --> 00:04:11,408
There is the REST API, the OpenAPI contract and the integration guide.

62
00:04:11,458 --> 00:04:16,232
The MyID and TAF SDKs are only in the master app.

63
00:04:16,282 --> 00:04:20,816
There is a short list of what an app may keep, and a longer list of what it never keeps.

64
00:04:20,866 --> 00:04:23,360
And the server applies every limit.

65
00:04:23,410 --> 00:04:27,800
With these foundations in place, Aziz can type his phone number for the first time.

66
00:04:27,850 --> 00:04:30,560
What happens between that number and level one?

67
00:04:30,610 --> 00:04:32,504
Next: registration.
